WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:08.301
To deny a conditional claim, describe a case in which its sufficient condition is true and its necessary condition is false.

2
00:00:08.601 --> 00:00:10.521
That case is a counterexample.

3
00:00:10.821 --> 00:00:13.232
Every licensed stall displays its permit.

4
00:00:13.532 --> 00:00:18.460
A licensed stall that does not display its permit is exactly what the claim forbids.

5
00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.341
One such stall makes the universal false.

6
00:00:21.641 --> 00:00:27.167
An unlicensed stall with a permit does not: the original rule made no claim about that stall.

7
00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:33.824
No permit displayed → not licensed merely restates the original through contraposition.

8
00:00:34.124 --> 00:00:38.625
Assumption negation and flaw testing often turn on finding an exception.

9
00:00:38.925 --> 00:00:44.579
Writing another universal rule can look rigorous while failing to contradict the original at all.

10
00:00:44.879 --> 00:00:50.041
Keep the original population, timing and every member of a joint sufficient side.

11
00:00:50.341 --> 00:00:54.224
A counterexample outside the stated group does not test the claim.

12
00:00:54.524 --> 00:00:57.041
Do not ask whether your denial sounds opposite.

13
00:00:57.341 --> 00:01:02.376
Ask whether the original rule and your proposed counterexample could both be true.

14
00:01:02.676 --> 00:01:05.279
If they could, you have not contradicted the rule.
